  <learnings>["An instrument that measures your own claim must have its failure modes audited for SYMMETRY. git merge-tree's exit-1 conflation meant every failure pointed the same direction — toward 'conflict', the favourable answer. A biased failure mode in a measurement tool is worse than a broken one, because it produces plausible data.", "Ship durability before compression, never after. gzip and the ENOSPC fix had to land together: a truncated deflate stream is unreadable where truncated JSON might partly parse, so compressing a non-atomically-written file makes the existing hole deeper while looking like an improvement.", "Guard the destination BEFORE committing to the seal, not at the write-back. I assumed the dirty check belonged at restoreTree and was wrong — guarding there means you have already sealed and then refuse to write, leaving the fabric ahead of the worktree with no clean recovery.", "Hold the commit on an unattributed change, even when it is almost certainly fine. +50 lines appeared in admit.ts from neither agent's ownership list; asking twice cost nothing and the answer turned out to be load-bearing (the value must be resolved inside admit's lock or it measures against a tip the verdict never judged).", "Report a per-unit number, not a total. `14.87 MB per strand` is actionable where `1.02 GB` is abstract — a team can multiply the first by their expected commit count and decide; the second just sounds large.", "A config line that carries a durability property deserves a test. The .gitignore un-ignore of the authoritative tip got a regression test, because a future ignore-file cleanup would otherwise silently drop replication of the only pointer that matters."]</learnings>
